From 81a4e748e096b9490ed4920ce02135d80f019c38 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: estravagancia <estravagancia@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 13:26:05 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] tools/hip: gfx1200-hipblaslt-100200.txt, 67 rows
 recalibrated with tune_hipblaslt on an RX 9070 XT (16 GiB, reports gfx1200 on
 the 7.10.0a20251120 wheels) against hipBLASLt 1.2.0; prompts measured +1.2 %
 at 4K and +2.0 % at 16K on that card, docs updated with what was and was not
 measured

---
 docs/AMD_HIP.md                         | 19 +++++--
 tests/terminal/test_hipblaslt_tuning.py |  2 +-
 tools/hip/gfx1200-hipblaslt-100200.txt  | 69 +++++++++++++++++++++++++
 3 files changed, 86 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)
 create mode 100644 tools/hip/gfx1200-hipblaslt-100200.txt

diff --git a/docs/AMD_HIP.md b/docs/AMD_HIP.md
index 9dd8aaaf..02d4a282 100644
--- a/docs/AMD_HIP.md
+++ b/docs/AMD_HIP.md
@@ -119,8 +119,8 @@ What was run on real hardware:
 - **Tuning tables (0.2.1):** each AMD package carries the hipBLASLt tuning tables of `tools/hip` that match the hipBLASLt it bundles, as
   `tuning/<arch>-hipblaslt-<version>.txt` (listed and hashed in the package manifest), and records that version as `hipblaslt_version` in its
   BUILD.json. Windows `rx9070` has the gfx1201 table, Windows `rdna3` the gfx1100 and gfx1151 ones (all hipBLASLt 1.5.0), Linux `rx7900` the
-  gfx1100 one (1.2.0), Linux `halo` the gfx1151 one (1.4.1). The RX 6000 packages (`rx6000`, `rdna2`, 0.2.2) have none, because hipBLASLt has no gfx1030 / gfx1031 kernels. Linux `rx9070` has none: its wheels carry hipBLASLt 1.2.0 and no table in `tools/hip`
-  is for that version (a table calibrated for it on an R9700 read prompts about 1% faster and decode 2% slower, so none is shipped).
+  gfx1100 one (1.2.0), Linux `halo` the gfx1151 one (1.4.1). The RX 6000 packages (`rx6000`, `rdna2`, 0.2.2) have none, because hipBLASLt has no gfx1030 / gfx1031 kernels. Linux `rx9070` has the gfx1200 one (1.2.0): the table tuned on an RX 9070 XT, which reports gfx1200 on those
+  wheels (they predate the gfx1200 / gfx1201 split); that package's gfx1201 card reaches it as a family sibling.
   The engine start picks the table for the card and uses it only when the package's hipBLASLt version is the table's; otherwise it logs
   why and the dense matrix products stay on plain hipBLAS. **A table changes which GEMM algorithm runs the prompt, so the rounding differs: the
   answers are not bit-identical to plain hipBLAS** (first-token KL 0.016 on an R9700, see below). Packaged engines therefore use the table by
@@ -545,7 +545,9 @@ an RX 9070 XT 16 GB and a Radeon AI PRO R9700 32 GB (both gfx1201), a Ryzen 9 39
   - Ship status of `tools/hip/gfx1201-hipblaslt-*.txt`: the gfx120X wheel's hipBLASLt is 1.2.0 (version number 100200); the
     tables here are 100202 and 100500, so the engine says "version mismatch ... using hipBLASEx" and runs plain hipBLAS. A
     table tuned on the R9700 for 100200 (32 rows, `tune_hipblaslt`) measured within noise end to end (20K prompt x1.035 vs
-    a noise arm x1.031), so none is shipped: the dense projections are ~10% of a 20K prompt here.
+    a noise arm x1.031), so none is shipped: the dense projections are ~10% of a 20K prompt here. A 1.2.0 table tuned
+    on an RX 9070 XT instead (67 rows, `tune_hipblaslt`; that card reports gfx1200 on these wheels) ships as
+    `gfx1200-hipblaslt-100200.txt`, and a gfx1201 card reaches it as a family sibling; its effect on an R9700 is not measured.
   - The prompt path's QSA top-k picks its kernel by the blocks a query actually has, not the cache's capacity (#337,
     bsorensen110): the same ids, on by default with AMD (NVIDIA keeps its capacity rule: there the 64K prompts read
     1-3% slower with it). `STRATA_SELECT_WMMA=1` (opt-in, gfx12) adds #337's
@@ -957,6 +959,17 @@ Shipped tables:
   valid: the engine falls back to hipBLASEx for an id the library rejects, and the test still passes. Run it with
   `STRATA_HIPBLASLT_VERBOSE=1` and look for `fallbacks=0` in its summary line, and recalibrate with
   `tune_hipblaslt` before using this table with a different 1.5.0 build.
+- `gfx1200-hipblaslt-100200.txt`: RX 9070 XT (gfx1201, 16 GiB, Ryzen 7 9800X3D), recalibrated with `tune_hipblaslt
+  --workspace-mib 32` from the cases of `gfx1200-hipblaslt-100202.txt` (67 rows: 28 dense GEMM geometries at their T
+  buckets) against the hipBLASLt 1.2.0 (version number 100200) of the 7.10.0a20251120 gfx120X wheels setup installs for
+  RDNA4. Those wheels predate the gfx1200 / gfx1201 split and the card reports gfx1200 on them, which is the name the
+  file carries; setup stages it in the Linux `rx9070` package, and a gfx1201 card of that package reaches it as a family
+  sibling. End to end on engine 0.1.47 with IQ3_XXS on that box, bench runs from 2026-10-21 compared the same engine
+  with and without the table: prompt 4K 1,965.9 -> 1,989.5 tok/s (+1.2 %) and 16K 2,398.4 -> 2,445.9 (+2.0 %), chat decode
+  71.4 -> 70.6; the two runs ran at ctx 100000 and 105000, so their decode difference is confounded with the context. The
+  R9700's own 1.2.0 tuning (above) measured decode 2.1 % slower there and no 1.2.0 table stays shipped for gfx1201; what
+  this table does on an R9700 is not measured.
+
 - `gfx1151-hipblaslt-100400.txt`: Radeon 8060S (Ryzen AI Max+ 395, gfx1151, 128 GB; Fedora 44, kernel 7.2.8),
   calibrated with the ROCm 7.14.0a20260608 wheels that setup installs for gfx1151 since 0.1.40.2 (#1267): their
   hipBLASLt is 1.4.0 (version number 100400), so neither `gfx1151-hipblaslt-100401.txt` nor `-100500.txt` applied and
diff --git a/tests/terminal/test_hipblaslt_tuning.py b/tests/terminal/test_hipblaslt_tuning.py
index ae2f0b9e..b5096666 100644
--- a/tests/terminal/test_hipblaslt_tuning.py
+++ b/tests/terminal/test_hipblaslt_tuning.py
@@ -27,7 +27,7 @@ import stage_tuning  # noqa: E402
 PACKAGES = {
     ("windows-x86_64", "rx9070"): (("gfx1200", "gfx1201"), 100500, {"gfx1201"}),
     ("windows-x86_64", "rdna3"): (("gfx1100", "gfx1101", "gfx1102", "gfx1151"), 100500, {"gfx1100", "gfx1151"}),
-    ("linux-x86_64", "rx9070"): (("gfx1200", "gfx1201"), 100200, set()),                    # no table for 1.2.0 on RDNA4 (docs/AMD_HIP.md)
+    ("linux-x86_64", "rx9070"): (("gfx1200", "gfx1201"), 100200, {"gfx1200"}),            # the RX 9070 XT table for 1.2.0; the package's gfx1201 card reaches it as a sibling
     ("linux-x86_64", "rx7900"): (("gfx1100", "gfx1101", "gfx1102"), 100200, {"gfx1100"}),
     ("linux-x86_64", "halo"): (("gfx1151",), 100401, {"gfx1151"}),
     # 0.2.2 RX 6000 packages: hipBLASLt ships no gfx1030 / gfx1031 kernels at any version, so no table, plain hipBLAS
diff --git a/tools/hip/gfx1200-hipblaslt-100200.txt b/tools/hip/gfx1200-hipblaslt-100200.txt
